article="""<p><strong>DRAFT — NOT PUBLISHED</strong></p><h1>How to Compare Insulation Quotes Without Comparing Unlike Work</h1>
<p>Two insulation offers can show the same headline R-value but cover different zones, preparation and records. Start by making the object of the purchase explicit. This checklist helps prepare questions; it does not specify an assembly or tell you to disturb material.</p>
<h2>Ask which R-value claim you are comparing</h2><p>The <a href="https://consumer.ftc.gov/articles/what-know-when-youre-buying-home-insulation">FTC consumer guide</a> explains R-value as resistance to heat flow and discusses product fact sheets. Ask whether the number in the offer describes the product, an added layer or a proposed installed result. Keep the actual product information beside the exact zone. A sales label is not evidence that a particular home was assessed.</p>
<h2>Free six-row comparison checklist</h2><p>Copy these rows into a table with columns for A, B, source and unanswered question:</p><ol><li>Boundary: which drawing, assembly and measured area?</li><li>Result: what product and installed-result basis is stated?</li><li>Preparation: what is retained, removed, protected and disposed of?</li><li>Related work: is assessed air sealing included or excluded?</li><li>Price rule: what minimums, quantities and allowances control?</li><li>Completion: which product, scope and verification records will be supplied?</li></ol>
<h2>A free area check</h2><p>Invented scope arithmetic: a 1,250-square-foot boundary has two excluded zones of 100 and 150 square feet, with 40 square feet overlapping. Combined exclusion is 100 + 150 − 40 = 210; remaining modeled area is 1,040. Subtracting both whole exclusions gives 1,000, a 40-square-foot understatement. Ask the provider to reconcile the drawing. The arithmetic does not decide which actual zones need work.</p>
<h2>Keep insulation and supporting work visible</h2><p><a href="https://www.energystar.gov/saveathome/seal_insulate">ENERGY STAR's planning page</a> treats air sealing and insulation together and notes that insulation is not interchangeable across every location. Ask the qualified provider to define their assessed relationship in the offer. Do not assume a material quantity includes all related work.</p>
<p><a href="https://www.epa.gov/mold/brief-guide-mold-moisture-and-your-home">EPA's moisture guide</a> emphasizes moisture control. If the proposal depends on resolving an observed condition, record the responsible professional and readiness evidence before dependent work. This article gives no cleanup, testing, sealing or installation steps.</p>
<h2>End with a question the installer can answer</h2><p>Try 'Which boundary does your price cover?', 'How does the minimum apply?' or 'Does this replace an included allowance?' Preserve the original quote; an owner's calculation is not the provider's revised offer.</p>
<p>The <a href="/calculators/contractor-quote-comparison">free comparison tool</a> helps with general scope. The <a href="/books/drafts/insulation-product.html">draft Insulation Quote &amp; Scope Workbook</a> adds all four checked cases, six worksheets, 30 note fields and two filled records across 25 pages. Proposed £7.99 GBP, not implemented or offered for sale.</p>
<p>Primary sources checked October 31, 2026. All examples are fictional. No actual installed result, market price, incentive, savings, reader outcome or purchase demand is claimed. Proposed final article URL: https://homecostengine.com/blog/compare-insulation-quotes-without-unlike-work — not published.</p>"""
(out/'insulation-article.html').write_text(head+article+'</main></body></html>')
